/-!
# The Jacobian criterion for a node

Let `k` be a field, `A = k[[x_0, …, x_{n-1}]]` and `I ⊆ 𝔪_A` an ideal generated by a finite
family `f`. Stacks 0C4C asserts that `A/I` is a nodal `k`-algebra exactly when it is reduced —
equivalently, has depth one — and the `(n-1) × (n-1)` minors of the Jacobian matrix of `f`
generate the maximal ideal of `A/I`.

`Stacks.Curves.isNodalAlgebra_tfae_of_jacobianMinorIdeal_iff` reduces that equivalence to the
single statement that the Jacobian-minor ideal of Tag 0C4B at `n - 1` is the maximal ideal of
`A/I` exactly when `A/I` carries a nondegenerate quadratic relation on its cotangent space, and
`Stacks.Curves.jacobianMinorIdealModulo_eq_maximalIdeal_iff_hasNodalQuadraticRelation` proves that
statement for `n = 2` and `I ⊆ 𝔪_A ^ 2`. This file proves it in general, by the reduction the
source performs, and assembles the criterion.

## Main results

All of the following live in the `Stacks.Curves` namespace.

* `jacobianMinorIdealModulo_eq_maximalIdeal_iff_hasNodalQuadraticRelation_of_le_sq` — the
  Jacobian-versus-relation equivalence for any number of variables and a defining ideal inside
  `𝔪 ^ 2`. Only `n = 2` has content: for every other number of variables both sides are false,
  because the cotangent space of the quotient then does not have dimension two.
* `exists_partial_rsop_of_ideal` — an ideal inside the maximal ideal is generated modulo
  `𝔪 ^ 2` by part of a regular system of parameters.
* `minorIdeal_sup_eq_of_generators` — Tag 0C4B pulled back along the quotient map: the ideal of
  minors joined with the defining ideal does not depend on the generating family.
* `jacobianMinorIdealModulo_eq_maximalIdeal_iff_hasNodalQuadraticRelation_of_le_maximalIdeal` —
  **the reduction**: the Jacobian-versus-relation equivalence for an arbitrary `n` and an
  arbitrary ideal inside the maximal ideal.
* `isNodalAlgebra_jacobianMinorIdeal_tfae` — **the Jacobian criterion**, Tag 0C4C.

## Implementation notes

The reduction runs in three steps, and each is a change of presentation rather than new
mathematics.

First, part of a regular system of parameters lying in `I` and generating `I` modulo `𝔪 ^ 2` is
extended to a full regular system of parameters `y` by
`Stacks.MvPowerSeries.RegularParameters.extend_partial_rsop_field`. Tag 0C4B
(`Stacks.Curves.jacobianMinorIdealModulo_eq`) says the Jacobian-minor ideal may be computed in
the parameters `y` and with any other generating family at the same time, so no chain rule is
needed here: conjugating by the coordinate automorphism of `y` replaces `I` by an ideal `I'`
containing the first `r` variables and contained in those variables plus `𝔪 ^ 2`.

Second, adding the first `r` variables to the generating family puts the Jacobian matrix, after
reindexing rows and columns along `finSumFinEquiv`, in the block shape `fromBlocks 1 Q 0 M`. The
*lower left* block is zero for any choice of the remaining generators — differentiating a
variable in a different variable gives zero — so the Schur complement of
`Stacks.Curves.minorIdeal_fromBlocks_isUnit_det` is `M` itself, and the `(n-1)`-minors of the
Jacobian are the `(n-r-1)`-minors of `M`. No normalisation of the remaining generators, and no
correction term, is required.

Third, killing the first `r` variables (`Stacks.MvPowerSeries.RegularParameters.initialVarsAlgHom`)
  carries `M` to the
Jacobian matrix of the image family in `n - r` variables, because that substitution intertwines
the partial derivatives (`Stacks.MvPowerSeries.RegularParameters.initialVarsAlgHom_pderiv`). Both
  the ideal of minors and the
maximal ideal contain the kernel of that substitution, so the Jacobian condition upstairs and
downstairs are equivalent; and the image ideal lies in the square of the maximal ideal, which is
the case already proved. The two quotients are identified by a single isomorphism, along which
`Stacks.Curves.hasNodalQuadraticRelation_congr` transports the cotangent-space condition.

The case `n = r`, where no variables survive, is treated separately: the Jacobian matrix then has
the whole identity matrix among its columns, so its ideal of `(n-1)`-minors is the unit ideal and
the Jacobian condition fails, while the quotient has zero-dimensional cotangent space and carries
no nodal relation.

